WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.180
The worm has two killers, a brake, and a protein that releases it.

2
00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:09.420
Does a mammal use the same parts?

3
00:00:10.483 --> 00:00:12.583
Cloning the worm genes gave the answer.

4
00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:18.333
In 1993, ced-3 turned out to resemble a mammalian protease.

5
00:00:19.317 --> 00:00:22.897
That protease belongs to a family later named caspases.

6
00:00:23.950 --> 00:00:26.730
Caspases cut with a cysteine in their active site.

7
00:00:27.700 --> 00:00:30.300
They cut just after an aspartate in their target.

8
00:00:31.283 --> 00:00:33.963
Caspases are made as inactive precursors.

9
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:38.713
Initiator caspases switch on when they are gathered onto a platform.

10
00:00:39.817 --> 00:00:42.897
They then cut and activate executioner caspases.

11
00:00:43.950 --> 00:00:48.310
Executioners cut hundreds of cellular proteins, and take the cell apart.

12
00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:54.320
In 1994, ced-9 turned out to be a relative of human Bcl-2.

13
00:00:55.863 --> 00:00:58.363
Bcl-2 is a protein that keeps cells alive.

14
00:00:59.383 --> 00:01:01.663
It belongs to the BCL-2 family.

15
00:01:02.603 --> 00:01:06.043
Its members decide whether mitochondria release cytochrome c.

16
00:01:07.033 --> 00:01:08.333
Some members protect.

17
00:01:09.433 --> 00:01:10.873
Others, such as Bax, promote death.

18
00:01:11.653 --> 00:01:15.293
The BH3-only members, like EGL-1, promote death too.

19
00:01:16.173 --> 00:01:19.013
That short domain is their only shared family feature.

20
00:01:20.017 --> 00:01:23.917
And ced-4 proved related to a mammalian protein, Apaf-1.

21
00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:27.620
So each worm part has a mammalian counterpart.

22
00:01:28.420 --> 00:01:30.040
But read where the match fails, too.

23
00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.230
First, a question.

24
00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.030
Which mammalian part has no clear counterpart in the worm?

25
00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:46.650
Bax. It is a gate in the outer mitochondrial membrane.

26
00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:49.090
The worm has no clear counterpart.

27
00:01:50.150 --> 00:01:51.730
Now read where the match fails.

28
00:01:52.733 --> 00:01:54.953
The worm brake holds CED-4 directly.

29
00:01:55.833 --> 00:01:57.893
The mammalian brake guards the mitochondria.

30
00:01:58.867 --> 00:02:00.727
Apaf-1 needs cytochrome c.

31
00:02:01.507 --> 00:02:04.947
There is no clear role for cytochrome c in worm apoptosis.

32
00:02:05.967 --> 00:02:11.607
EGL-1 resembles BH3-only proteins, such as Bim, only in that domain.

33
00:02:12.717 --> 00:02:16.097
So EGL-1 is best called a functional counterpart.

34
00:02:17.117 --> 00:02:22.037
And the worm's main caspase, CED-3, maps onto several mammalian caspases.

35
00:02:23.097 --> 00:02:25.717
Mammals split the initiator and executioner roles.

36
00:02:26.750 --> 00:02:33.050
In 2002, a Nobel Prize honoured discoveries concerning the genetic regulation of

37
00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.570
organ development and programmed cell death.

38
00:02:36.500 --> 00:02:39.120
Here is the first part of this lesson's one idea.

39
00:02:40.133 --> 00:02:44.153
The killers are already built; a survival signal keeps brakes on them.
